  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are positive numbers such that \\(a^b=b^a\\) and \\(b=9a,\\) then the value of \\(a\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(9\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac19\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt[9]{9}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt[3]{9}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt[4]{3}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Substituting \\(b=9a\\) makes the two exponents share a factor of \\(a\\):\n\\[\na^{9a}=(9a)^a.\n\\]\nSince \\(a>0\\), both bases are positive and \\(1/a\\) is defined. Raise both sides to the power \\(1/a\\) to remove that common factor from the exponents:\n\\[\na^9=9a.\n\\]\nThis operation is reversible by raising both sides to the power \\(a\\). We may also divide by \\(a\\), because it is positive, obtaining\n\\[\na^8=9.\n\\]\nThe unique positive solution is\n\\[\na=9^{1/8}=3^{1/4}=\\boxed{\\sqrt[4]{3}}.\n\\]\nAll transformations are reversible for \\(a>0\\), so this value satisfies the original conditions with \\(b=9a\\). The answer is choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
